# 3. PRODUCT PRINCIPLES

Follow these principles throughout the implementation.

## 3.1 Student-first design

Make the platform easy to understand for a secondary school student using a phone for the first time.

Avoid unnecessary professional jargon.

Explain unfamiliar concepts in plain English.

Use practical examples relevant to Zambia.

## 3.2 Low-data accessibility

The application must load quickly on slow internet connections.

Avoid large background videos, unnecessary animations, oversized images, and heavy UI dependencies.

Use SVG icons, optimized assets, and lightweight CSS wherever possible.

Load secondary content only when necessary.

Make the main career catalogue usable without repeatedly requesting AI-generated responses.

## 3.3 Honest career guidance

Do not promise that a student will succeed in a career.

Do not present a recommendation score as a scientifically validated probability.

Clearly distinguish between verified information, general guidance, illustrative examples, and unverified information awaiting confirmation.

Never fabricate official qualifications, admission requirements, scholarships, current vacancies, salaries, or course availability.

## 3.4 Inclusive recommendations

Never rank or restrict careers based on gender, disability, religion, ethnicity, family income, province, or other protected or irrelevant personal characteristics.

A student's financial circumstances may inform optional affordability guidance, but must never determine whether the student is considered suitable for a career.

Do not use stereotypes such as assuming that boys should pursue engineering or girls should pursue nursing.

Present multiple possibilities and encourage students to explore alternatives.

## 3.5 Progressive development

Build a functional first version using stable, understandable technology.

Keep the architecture modular so that secure authentication, a hosted database, an AI provider, counselor tools, and verified opportunity feeds can be introduced without rewriting the entire application.

